March 9th 2016 a Japanese weather  satellite orbiting high above the  Pacific Ocean captures a gigantic dark  shape moving across the earth looks kind  of like the spacecraft from the  Independence Day movie right before it  obliterates the city this it's  gargantuan  the black shape is almost 3,000 miles  wide the satellite tracks it moving  rapidly across the surface of the ocean  there's this giant black area almost the  size of Australia you got to wonder  what's causing that  the satellite tracks the dark shape for  four hours but then it mysteriously  disappears a clue to the mystery lies in  the date the image was captured the  shadow occurred during a rare and  inspiring celestial event  could this be responsible for the  darkening over the Pacific  on March 9 2016 there was a solar  eclipse this is when the moon comes in  between the Sun and the earth kinda like  my fist coming between me and the camera  and a shadow then appears on the earth  the dark shape is nothing less than the  shadow of the Moon as seen from space it  occurred over the Pacific Ocean and over  Indonesia you can see the shadow moving  across the surface of the earth during a  total eclipse the moon casts a shadow  around 3,000 miles across that travels  at around 2,000 miles per hour as it  sweeps across the surface of the earth  during his time on the ISS astronaut  Leroy Chiao was one of the few people to  film the spectacle from space we were  told by the ground that there was going  to be an eclipse event and so we were  kind of ready for it and as I looked at  the window I saw this dark spot moving  pretty rapidly across the earth it did  feel a little eerie because you know you  there's not very often that you get to  observe it and eclipse event from space  so you don't really appreciate the how  big that shadow is that's moving across  the earth it's amazing to see what an  eclipse looks like from space  you  you
 

